//// Decision 95 — `@Context` ⊃ `@Future` ⊃ `@Result`, so `await` is legal in a
//// `#[@context]` body. `run/effect_chain.bp` carries `use` and `try`, the two
//// capabilities every backend runs the same way; this cell carries the third,
//// which was left out of that one because commonJS lowered a `#[@context]`
//// body to a plain `function` and the emitted `await` was a JS `SyntaxError`
//// — the module did not load at all, while erlang and wasm printed the value.
////
//// Both shapes decision 88 and decision 96 allow for the return are here: the
//// explicit wrapper `-> @Context<Element, i32>`, and a type that IMPLEMENTS
//// `@Context` (`-> Element`, the component). They are two paths through the
//// emitter, not one.
////
//// Every value is printed from INSIDE the awaiting body. That is the cell's
//// one piece of care and the reason it reads the way it does: commonJS hands
//// a suspending function's CALLER a promise — exactly as it already does for
//// `#[@future]`, measured — while erlang and wasm hand it the value, so a
//// `@print` at the call site would compare a promise with `41`. What every
//// target does agree on is the body: it runs, in order, with the awaited value
//// in hand. The `#[@context]` prints therefore come last, after `main`'s own.

type Element(count: i32) implement @Context<Element, Element>

fn state(initial: i32) -> @Context<Element, i32> {
    return initial;
}

#[@future]
fn answer(n: i32) -> @Future<i32> {
    return n;
}

// The explicit wrapper: `await` two levels down the chain.
#[@context]
fn page(n: i32) -> @Context<Element, i32> {
    val v = await answer(n);
    @print(v);
    return v;
}

// The component shape — `use` and `await` in one body.
#[@context]
fn Widget(n: i32) -> Element {
    val c = use state(1);
    val v = await answer(n);
    @print(c + v);
    return Element(count: c + v);
}

// A `#[@context]` body that awaits NOTHING keeps answering its value directly,
// on every target: its caller reads the field off what it returns.
#[@context]
fn Plain(n: i32) -> Element {
    val c = use state(1);
    return Element(count: c + n);
}

pub fn main() {
    @print(Plain(40).count);
    page(41);
    Widget(41);
}
